WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.685 --> 00:00:05.068
Welcome to "Ear Biscuits",

00:00:05.068 --> 00:00:07.318
the podcast, where two lifelong friends

00:00:07.318 --> 00:00:09.963
talk about life for a
long time, I'm Rhett.

00:00:09.963 --> 00:00:11.078
And I'm Link.

00:00:11.078 --> 00:00:14.577
This week at the round
table of dim lighting

00:00:14.577 --> 00:00:19.023
we're hearing about your
near-death experiences.

00:00:19.023 --> 00:00:19.856
Yes.

00:00:19.856 --> 00:00:22.039
We're taking advantage of this line,

00:00:22.039 --> 00:00:24.009
we got this phone call line.

00:00:24.009 --> 00:00:24.842
This phone call line.

00:00:24.842 --> 00:00:25.675
A hotline,

00:00:25.675 --> 00:00:26.558
Man, I love it.

00:00:26.558 --> 00:00:27.391
I love people's voices.

00:00:27.391 --> 00:00:28.224
1-888.

00:00:28.224 --> 00:00:30.207
EARPOD1.

00:00:30.207 --> 00:00:31.936
I love people's voices.

00:00:31.936 --> 00:00:33.582
All you gotta do is call in

00:00:33.582 --> 00:00:36.619
when we put a prompt
on the Twitter machine.

00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:37.452
Yeah.

00:00:37.452 --> 00:00:38.531
So, and these are literally-

00:00:38.531 --> 00:00:39.412
We have a Twitter machine.

00:00:39.412 --> 00:00:41.123
Near-death experiences.

00:00:41.123 --> 00:00:45.672
And what I mean by that
is like people almost died

00:00:45.672 --> 00:00:46.546
and nearly died.

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:50.560
We asked for ones that were
funny or bizarre specifically.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.935
And we got, and maybe
there's a serious one

00:00:52.935 --> 00:00:54.393
or two in there too.

00:00:54.393 --> 00:00:57.193
I am embarking on a journey.

00:00:57.193 --> 00:00:58.524
I'm making an investment.

00:00:58.524 --> 00:01:00.265
I'm learning something new.

00:01:00.265 --> 00:01:03.971
I am currently being
trained to do something

00:01:03.971 --> 00:01:05.640
to be certified.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:06.473
TaeKwonDo?

00:01:06.473 --> 00:01:07.306
And nope.

00:01:07.306 --> 00:01:11.721
And it could lead to a
near-death experience.

00:01:11.721 --> 00:01:13.738
It could lead to a death experience.

00:01:13.738 --> 00:01:14.571
Parachuting?

00:01:14.571 --> 00:01:17.914
But it could also lead
to like an eyeopening,

00:01:17.914 --> 00:01:20.937
heart-opening, life-changing.

00:01:20.937 --> 00:01:21.943
Heart-opening?

00:01:21.943 --> 00:01:23.434
Yeah.

00:01:23.434 --> 00:01:25.207
Future-defining experience,

00:01:25.207 --> 00:01:28.736
relationship defining experience for me

00:01:28.736 --> 00:01:32.074
and a significant other in my life.

00:01:32.074 --> 00:01:33.216
You're really building this up.

00:01:33.216 --> 00:01:34.049
Yeah.

00:01:34.049 --> 00:01:35.223
So, I think this is the right episode

00:01:35.223 --> 00:01:37.108
to let you know.

00:01:37.108 --> 00:01:39.808
You gonna start playing
Magic, The Gathering?

00:01:39.808 --> 00:01:42.716
No, I'm open to it, but that's not it.

00:01:42.716 --> 00:01:45.049
I am learning to scuba dive.

00:01:46.992 --> 00:01:47.825
Ah.

00:01:47.825 --> 00:01:49.449
I am becoming scuba certified.

00:01:49.449 --> 00:01:51.455
I knew that, I was just playing dumb.

00:01:51.455 --> 00:01:52.288
Yeah.

00:01:53.719 --> 00:01:56.688
Ever since, and I just wanna tell you,

00:01:56.688 --> 00:02:01.582
I've been waiting to tell you
about the process of training.

00:02:01.582 --> 00:02:03.139
Get you wet?

00:02:03.139 --> 00:02:04.490
Back when we went on tour,

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.109
we were in Australia and we
were like, "You know what,

00:02:06.109 --> 00:02:07.464
when you're coming back from Australia,

00:02:07.464 --> 00:02:09.929
there's that place called Fiji."

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:10.762
Yeah.

00:02:10.762 --> 00:02:11.595
Might as well fly over it.

00:02:11.595 --> 00:02:15.291
And we went scuba diving that one day.

00:02:15.291 --> 00:02:16.124
No, snorkeling.
No, we didn't.

00:02:16.124 --> 00:02:17.277
We went snorkeling.

00:02:17.277 --> 00:02:19.027
When none of us are certified.

00:02:19.027 --> 00:02:23.360
And I had just a magical
experience under the water,

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:29.358
like swimming in the
reef in the tidal zone

00:02:29.358 --> 00:02:32.543
on this island that this boat took us to.

00:02:32.543 --> 00:02:35.543
And it was just like, so meditative.

00:02:36.494 --> 00:02:38.877
I thought I was, and I
think I talked about it

00:02:38.877 --> 00:02:39.710
on the podcast.

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:40.543
Yeah.

00:02:40.543 --> 00:02:42.233
As a highlight of that vacation years ago

00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:44.900
that I was gonna cry in my mask.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.083
You can't do that 'cause you'll die.

00:02:48.083 --> 00:02:50.500
No, it was just so awesome.

00:02:52.343 --> 00:02:56.073
And then that's when I
started to think, "Man,

00:02:56.073 --> 00:02:58.410
I'd really like to scuba dive."

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.026
And then I start talking to Chase,

00:03:00.026 --> 00:03:01.900
you know Chase is scuba certified?

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:02.733
Well, okay.

00:03:02.733 --> 00:03:03.566
So I'm having some conversations with him.

00:03:03.566 --> 00:03:04.786
Let me interject.

00:03:04.786 --> 00:03:08.462
Because when you started
talking about scuba diving,

00:03:08.462 --> 00:03:12.369
you talked about it in
a way that there was a,

00:03:12.369 --> 00:03:15.709
there seemed to be a heavy
dose of fear involved.

00:03:15.709 --> 00:03:16.667
And every time you brought it up,

00:03:16.667 --> 00:03:18.721
it was like you were
stepping across a precipice

00:03:18.721 --> 00:03:21.446
into like a gauntlet of
sorts, so explain that.

00:03:21.446 --> 00:03:25.113
Because I have a fear of being underwater.

00:03:27.919 --> 00:03:29.095
That's the problem.

00:03:29.095 --> 00:03:31.778
And specifically, holding my breath.

00:03:31.778 --> 00:03:35.874
I mean, I jump in my
pool and I go underwater,

00:03:35.874 --> 00:03:36.707
hold my breath.

00:03:36.707 --> 00:03:38.527
I'm swimming around.

00:03:38.527 --> 00:03:42.998
I'm like very much aware
that there's this timer

00:03:42.998 --> 00:03:44.975
going off in my body.

00:03:44.975 --> 00:03:47.743
That's like if you don't get to the place

00:03:47.743 --> 00:03:50.876
where you can breathe air
soon you're gonna die.

00:03:50.876 --> 00:03:54.072
It's an immediate feeling for me.

00:03:54.072 --> 00:03:56.407
Well, it's an important
survival mechanism.

00:03:56.407 --> 00:03:58.906
But, you know, and I
always chalked it up to,

00:03:58.906 --> 00:04:00.490
I was never a pool boy.

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:02.037
I was never, you were a pool boy,

00:04:02.037 --> 00:04:04.403
you went to the Keith Hills pool.

00:04:04.403 --> 00:04:06.365
Growing up, did a lot of swimming.

00:04:06.365 --> 00:04:08.758
I did very little swimming
in a pool environment

00:04:08.758 --> 00:04:10.139
where you like, hold your breath

00:04:10.139 --> 00:04:11.969
and you get comfortable
holding your breath.

00:04:11.969 --> 00:04:13.957
You were mostly a bathtub swimmer.

00:04:13.957 --> 00:04:14.790
Yeah.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:15.685
Or like a river swimmer.

00:04:15.685 --> 00:04:17.207
And you don't spend a lot of time on,

00:04:17.207 --> 00:04:20.523
you're more of wading and treading water.

00:04:20.523 --> 00:04:22.161
No ocean swimming?

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:22.994
Not a lot.

00:04:22.994 --> 00:04:25.643
I mean, ocean, it's more like body surfing

00:04:25.643 --> 00:04:27.288
and stuff like that,
you're on the surface.

00:04:27.288 --> 00:04:29.456
I didn't do a bunch of like...

00:04:29.456 --> 00:04:30.743
Oh, the diving in the ocean.

00:04:30.743 --> 00:04:31.576
That's the fun part.

00:04:31.576 --> 00:04:34.368
Like when I would go to
frigging the Keith Hills pool

00:04:34.368 --> 00:04:36.437
for the pool parties,
they would do that thing

00:04:36.437 --> 00:04:37.295
where everybody would-

00:04:37.295 --> 00:04:38.128
Cross the pool.

00:04:38.128 --> 00:04:41.226
Cross the pool and be one guy
in there trying to tag you

00:04:41.226 --> 00:04:44.517
and you and a bunch of other
people could just dive in,

00:04:44.517 --> 00:04:46.479
swim to the very bottom
of the pool and come up

00:04:46.479 --> 00:04:48.936
on the other side and never get tagged.

00:04:48.936 --> 00:04:50.575
And here I am like flailing around on top.

00:04:50.575 --> 00:04:52.263
I can swim.

00:04:52.263 --> 00:04:54.514
But something about being underwater.

00:04:54.514 --> 00:04:55.661
You're not a submarine, man.

00:04:55.661 --> 00:04:59.048
My mom is afraid of
swimming, as a kid she dove

00:04:59.048 --> 00:05:02.850
into a pool and hit her head,
and then she never swam again.

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:05.036
Well maybe her DNA was
altered when that happened.

00:05:05.036 --> 00:05:07.648
And it got passed to you somehow,
that can happen, you know,

00:05:07.648 --> 00:05:08.481
DNA can be altered by events.

00:05:08.481 --> 00:05:09.698
Maybe that's what it is.

00:05:09.698 --> 00:05:12.191
I didn't experience any trauma,

00:05:12.191 --> 00:05:16.887
but the Fiji snorkeling
experience was so formative for me

00:05:16.887 --> 00:05:19.832
that I was like, I wanna do this.

00:05:19.832 --> 00:05:21.709
I wanna be able to go under that water

00:05:21.709 --> 00:05:23.958
and not have to come up.

00:05:23.958 --> 00:05:25.458
It's a great idea.

00:05:26.571 --> 00:05:31.415
And plus I think I'm gonna
have to overcome this fear.

00:05:31.415 --> 00:05:33.056
And then I tried to talk Christy into it.

00:05:33.056 --> 00:05:37.076
She wasn't going for it,
but Lincoln was up for it.

00:05:37.076 --> 00:05:39.486
I was like, Lincoln, "We're going,

00:05:39.486 --> 00:05:42.374
we're planning this big trip to Hawaii."

00:05:42.374 --> 00:05:45.663
I was like, "Dude, we're
gonna scuba dive in Hawaii."

00:05:45.663 --> 00:05:46.496
And he was like, "All right.

00:05:46.496 --> 00:05:47.342
Yeah, I'll do it with you."

00:05:47.342 --> 00:05:51.608
This is also maybe the
plot of White Lotus.

00:05:51.608 --> 00:05:54.630
Did that enter into your...

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:55.463
Yeah.

00:05:55.463 --> 00:05:56.296
The teenage-

00:05:56.296 --> 00:05:57.129
The father, son.

00:05:57.129 --> 00:05:58.579
Yeah, they actually did that too.

00:05:58.579 --> 00:06:00.434
He's like, "Let's get
scuba certified, man."

00:06:00.434 --> 00:06:03.075
Well, I'd made up my mind before then-

00:06:03.075 --> 00:06:04.019
But that put you over the top.

00:06:04.019 --> 00:06:05.509
I didn't put any plans in place.

00:06:05.509 --> 00:06:07.916
And then when I watched
White Lotus, I was like,

00:06:07.916 --> 00:06:09.343
"I'm gonna put these plans in place.

00:06:09.343 --> 00:06:12.188
See how this teenager's life was changed.

00:06:12.188 --> 00:06:15.203
Like he found his calling in the ocean."

00:06:15.203 --> 00:06:16.078
Spoiler.

00:06:16.078 --> 00:06:18.681
Oh, well it's a nice storyline,

00:06:18.681 --> 00:06:20.275
but I wouldn't call it a spoiler.

00:06:20.275 --> 00:06:21.834
I mean, you're the one
who's sensitive to spoilers.

00:06:21.834 --> 00:06:22.667
I'm just joking.

00:06:22.667 --> 00:06:25.897
And so I said, "All
right, I'm gonna do this."

00:06:25.897 --> 00:06:27.859
Started talking to
Chase about the process.

00:06:27.859 --> 00:06:30.563
He made a recommendation
to Hollywood Divers,

00:06:30.563 --> 00:06:34.896
shout out to Hal and the
team that I linked up with.

00:06:36.583 --> 00:06:39.092
You gotta take an online course.

00:06:39.092 --> 00:06:40.379
Because Lincoln and I
were doing it together

00:06:40.379 --> 00:06:42.730
it took a number of months
for us to get through it.

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:44.694
You watch a video, you read some stuff,

00:06:44.694 --> 00:06:45.799
you answer some questions.

00:06:45.799 --> 00:06:46.801
If you don't get it right.

00:06:46.801 --> 00:06:48.491
You gotta answer all the questions again.

00:06:48.491 --> 00:06:50.597
But there's like five questions at a time.

00:06:50.597 --> 00:06:53.223
And it, I mean, it was
probably cumulatively

00:06:53.223 --> 00:06:55.121
over three hours' worth of stuff.

00:06:55.121 --> 00:06:57.682
And then at the end, there's
like a 70-question test.

00:06:57.682 --> 00:06:59.841
I'm like, "Lincoln you're, dude,

00:06:59.841 --> 00:07:02.026
you're used to taking tests.

00:07:02.026 --> 00:07:03.754
You show up and go to
school any day of a week.

00:07:03.754 --> 00:07:05.406
You're you like, 'All
right, another test.'

00:07:05.406 --> 00:07:07.433
Last test I took was a
freaking driving test.

00:07:07.433 --> 00:07:08.757
When I moved to California,

00:07:08.757 --> 00:07:10.489
before that when's the last test I took?"

00:07:10.489 --> 00:07:13.036
like, test-taking is a skill.

00:07:13.036 --> 00:07:13.881
So I was like, "Man,

00:07:13.881 --> 00:07:17.082
I'm gonna be out just because a freaking,

00:07:17.082 --> 00:07:18.458
I don't even know how to take tests.

00:07:18.458 --> 00:07:20.201
Like, I don't know how
to retain information."

00:07:20.201 --> 00:07:22.349
Did he beat you on the test?

00:07:22.349 --> 00:07:24.927
I let him take the 70-question test

00:07:24.927 --> 00:07:26.032
a little bit ahead of me.

00:07:26.032 --> 00:07:27.127
And he's moving fast.

00:07:27.127 --> 00:07:28.475
I'm like, "How are you moving so fast?

00:07:28.475 --> 00:07:29.334
You're gonna get these wrong.

00:07:29.334 --> 00:07:30.814
You're gonna fail."

00:07:30.814 --> 00:07:34.235
And then he passed with like a 73

00:07:34.235 --> 00:07:36.536
and then it showed him all of his answers.

00:07:36.536 --> 00:07:39.322
And I was like, well,
let me scroll over here.

00:07:39.322 --> 00:07:40.260
Oh, no.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:42.796
Just let me, you know, I don't want to...

00:07:42.796 --> 00:07:45.811
I just wanna like see if what I'm thinking

00:07:45.811 --> 00:07:47.644
is the right answer.

00:07:47.644 --> 00:07:50.716
Your certification is about
to be stripped from you, sir.

00:07:50.716 --> 00:07:53.438
I'm just saying, well, of
course, I didn't do it.

00:07:53.438 --> 00:07:54.292
I just thought about it.

00:07:54.292 --> 00:07:55.125
Okay.

00:07:55.125 --> 00:07:55.958
All right.

00:07:55.958 --> 00:07:56.791
Thanks for clarifying.

00:07:56.791 --> 00:07:58.734
I didn't go over there and actually verify

00:07:58.734 --> 00:08:01.442
that his answers agreed with mine,

00:08:01.442 --> 00:08:04.037
but I bet you that they
did if I would've checked.

00:08:04.037 --> 00:08:05.362
What'd you get on the test?

00:08:05.362 --> 00:08:06.610
A 91.

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:07.520
Okay.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:08.983
Well, okay.

00:08:08.983 --> 00:08:11.772
It helps to, you know what?

00:08:11.772 --> 00:08:13.103
I'm not even going to say anything.

00:08:13.103 --> 00:08:13.936
Yeah.

00:08:13.936 --> 00:08:14.769
It just helps to be knowledgeable.

00:08:14.769 --> 00:08:16.889
I don't want you to be
stripped of your license.

00:08:16.889 --> 00:08:21.806
But dude, I mean, they spend
very, very, very little time

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:26.851
in this course talking about
how amazing scuba diving is.

00:08:26.851 --> 00:08:31.269
Matter of fact, there's basically
no part of the curriculum

00:08:31.269 --> 00:08:33.319
that says, "Remember how awesome this is."

00:08:33.319 --> 00:08:35.065
So by the time I got to the end of it,

00:08:35.065 --> 00:08:36.582
all I learned was that like,

00:08:36.582 --> 00:08:38.574
you can die in a million different ways.

00:08:38.574 --> 00:08:42.639
You can mame your body and be in all types

00:08:42.639 --> 00:08:44.511
of internal pain.

00:08:44.511 --> 00:08:47.412
I mean, the one thing that
put me at ease was like,

00:08:47.412 --> 00:08:49.978
all right, I have a fear of
holding my breath underwater.

00:08:49.978 --> 00:08:52.748
But one of the first things
you learn in scuba class

00:08:52.748 --> 00:08:54.915
is never hold your breath.

00:08:55.798 --> 00:08:56.631
Yeah.

00:08:56.631 --> 00:08:57.464
You don't hold your breath.

00:08:57.464 --> 00:08:58.297
You're breathing the whole time.

00:08:58.297 --> 00:09:00.278
Because not just because you have air,

00:09:00.278 --> 00:09:02.576
but because the further you go down,

00:09:02.576 --> 00:09:05.008
the more pressure gets on your lungs.

00:09:05.008 --> 00:09:07.475
And if you hold your
breath under pressure,

00:09:07.475 --> 00:09:09.988
and then you start to ascend,

00:09:09.988 --> 00:09:12.016
your lungs become a closed system

00:09:12.016 --> 00:09:13.250
when you're holding your breath.

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:15.230
And then all those little balloons-

00:09:15.230 --> 00:09:16.555
It's like a paper bag.

00:09:16.555 --> 00:09:18.611
All those little balloons,
the air inside starts

00:09:18.611 --> 00:09:21.903
to expand your freaking lungs explode.

00:09:21.903 --> 00:09:22.760
That's not good.

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:23.593
Your freaking lungs explode.

00:09:23.593 --> 00:09:27.924
So you have to have a discipline
to be constantly breathing.

00:09:27.924 --> 00:09:31.257
How deep does that become an issue at?

00:09:32.127 --> 00:09:33.377
I don't know.

00:09:34.569 --> 00:09:37.492
They don't want you
knowing the exceptions.

00:09:37.492 --> 00:09:40.591
And maybe I did know, but
I can't remember that.

00:09:40.591 --> 00:09:41.536
But not pool depth?

00:09:41.536 --> 00:09:43.135
Not pool depth.

00:09:43.135 --> 00:09:44.827
No, not like eight or 10 feet.

00:09:44.827 --> 00:09:46.251
Okay.

00:09:46.251 --> 00:09:48.920
Don't ask me questions that then I'm like,

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:50.854
don't listen to any of my facts

00:09:50.854 --> 00:09:51.687
that I'm stating about scuba.

00:09:51.687 --> 00:09:53.714
Well, the one thing I'm a
little bit worried about

00:09:53.714 --> 00:09:55.419
is there's so many numbers involved

00:09:55.419 --> 00:09:56.860
and there's like math
and there's calculations.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:57.693
Oh gosh.

00:09:57.693 --> 00:09:58.526
Yeah.

00:09:58.526 --> 00:10:00.271
Because you got all this nitrogen-

00:10:00.271 --> 00:10:01.594
And your life depends on it.

00:10:01.594 --> 00:10:04.463
You get this nitrogen that has to off-gas.

00:10:04.463 --> 00:10:06.430
And so depending on how deep you are,

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:07.793
you have to do all these calculations

00:10:07.793 --> 00:10:08.753
and keep all these records.

00:10:08.753 --> 00:10:09.985
You're gonna need a laminated chart.

00:10:09.985 --> 00:10:12.170
Like they make for the gut check episodes

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:13.741
that you take down and dive with.

00:10:13.741 --> 00:10:15.535
Either that or like a dive computer

00:10:15.535 --> 00:10:16.704
that does it all for you.

00:10:16.704 --> 00:10:17.537
That exists?

00:10:17.537 --> 00:10:18.370
Yeah.

00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:19.203
Get that.

00:10:19.203 --> 00:10:20.169
I can rent one of those.

00:10:20.169 --> 00:10:22.550
But if you have to, but it's really, okay.

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:26.049
I can't dive past like
60-feet for more than an hour.

00:10:26.049 --> 00:10:27.608
I'm just throwing out numbers here.

00:10:27.608 --> 00:10:28.570
But then if you want to go-

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:30.302
You're gonna be 60 feet under the water?

00:10:30.302 --> 00:10:32.876
How does that make you feel?

00:10:32.876 --> 00:10:34.239
Well, I'll tell you how it made me feel

00:10:34.239 --> 00:10:36.153
when I did the next level of training.

00:10:36.153 --> 00:10:37.520
But yeah, they're talking about like,

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:40.185
you got all this nitrogen in your body

00:10:40.185 --> 00:10:43.981
and it has to off-gas and it
will accumulate over time.

00:10:43.981 --> 00:10:46.294
So if you don't wait
24 hours after a dive,

00:10:46.294 --> 00:10:49.258
you have to calculate how
deep you can go for how long

00:10:49.258 --> 00:10:53.369
in any subsequent dives in like
in the same 24-hour period.

00:10:53.369 --> 00:10:55.201
Think of all the people who
learned this the hard way.

00:10:55.201 --> 00:10:56.034
Yeah.

00:10:56.034 --> 00:11:00.083
So you don't come up too
and you don't get the bends.

00:11:00.083 --> 00:11:00.916
And then-

00:11:00.916 --> 00:11:02.440
Is that a TikTok dance?

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:05.600
It's the name of an album.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:08.241
You then move to the next phase,

00:11:08.241 --> 00:11:11.241
which is you get training in a pool.

00:11:12.272 --> 00:11:15.056
So we did this at our pool.

00:11:15.056 --> 00:11:18.639
It was just me, Lincoln,
and Matt, and Rey.

00:11:20.459 --> 00:11:22.361
They were supervising, the two of them

00:11:22.361 --> 00:11:24.610
were supervising our training.

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:27.950
And so we spent like four
and a half hours in my pool

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:31.798
this past weekend, learning how to dive.

00:11:31.798 --> 00:11:33.817
With the breathing apparatus,

00:11:33.817 --> 00:11:34.708
and the tanks, and everything?

00:11:34.708 --> 00:11:37.212
Yeah, with the full kit and caboodle.

00:11:37.212 --> 00:11:38.045
Wetsuit?

00:11:38.045 --> 00:11:39.466
Oh yeah, wetsuit and everything.

00:11:39.466 --> 00:11:42.103
Wetsuit, hood, boots, gloves,

00:11:42.103 --> 00:11:44.601
everything that I'm gonna
have for the next phase,

00:11:44.601 --> 00:11:47.771
which is going into the open
ocean water certification,

00:11:47.771 --> 00:11:50.854
which is two days, I'm doing
that this coming weekend.

00:11:50.854 --> 00:11:52.128
Oh gosh.

00:11:52.128 --> 00:11:53.000
I think I am.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.091
Nice knowing you.

00:11:54.091 --> 00:11:56.070
So this past weekend we get
in the pool and they're like,

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:58.478
first of all, it's like even snorkeling,

00:11:58.478 --> 00:12:01.022
when you put your face under the water,

00:12:01.022 --> 00:12:05.708
like everything in your being
is telling you don't breathe.

00:12:05.708 --> 00:12:07.632
I mean you're underwater, do not breathe.

00:12:07.632 --> 00:12:09.287
That's why you can throw a baby in water.

00:12:09.287 --> 00:12:10.120
Yeah.

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:12.756
It's instinct that you have to overcome

00:12:12.756 --> 00:12:15.526
that you have to start
breathing underwater.

00:12:15.526 --> 00:12:18.215
And it's pretty nerve-wracking.

00:12:18.215 --> 00:12:19.272
I mean, if you've ever snorkeled,

00:12:19.272 --> 00:12:21.702
there's like that moment of acclimation.

00:12:21.702 --> 00:12:25.452
That's like, "Oh, yes,
body, you can do this.

00:12:27.098 --> 00:12:29.148
Chill out brain, you can do this."

00:12:29.148 --> 00:12:31.804
Then you put on the regulator.

00:12:31.804 --> 00:12:33.321
Yeah.

00:12:33.321 --> 00:12:37.051
It's an amazing system that
you're putting this vest on

00:12:37.051 --> 00:12:40.781
that then it connects
to the tank behind you

00:12:40.781 --> 00:12:44.081
as well as you having a
regulator that you breathe

00:12:44.081 --> 00:12:47.608
the air out of the tank and
a backup octopus regulator

00:12:47.608 --> 00:12:49.691
that you can share with
somebody in an emergency

00:12:49.691 --> 00:12:51.057
or use yourself.

00:12:51.057 --> 00:12:52.430
Or share with an octopus.

00:12:52.430 --> 00:12:54.180
They don't need it.

00:12:55.048 --> 00:12:58.609
But then there's also a hose
that you hook to the vest

00:12:58.609 --> 00:13:01.898
because you wear this vest
and you put weights in it,

00:13:01.898 --> 00:13:03.235
depending on how much you weigh,

00:13:03.235 --> 00:13:05.447
you've gotta be able to sink.

00:13:05.447 --> 00:13:08.986
So you weigh yourself down
even more than just the tank

00:13:08.986 --> 00:13:11.594
and the vest, just ballast.

00:13:11.594 --> 00:13:12.782
And that's how you get back up.

00:13:12.782 --> 00:13:14.637
You fill the vest with more air.

00:13:14.637 --> 00:13:15.470
Yes.

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:16.975
You have this trigger over here.

00:13:16.975 --> 00:13:18.368
I always wondered about that.

00:13:18.368 --> 00:13:20.476
Instead of breathing the air in,

00:13:20.476 --> 00:13:24.976
it is pressurized and it
fills up your vest like a PFD

00:13:25.933 --> 00:13:28.635
and you can fill it up
a little bit at a time.

00:13:28.635 --> 00:13:29.468
And it's so interesting-

00:13:29.468 --> 00:13:30.937
And then you can let it
a little bit at a time.

00:13:30.937 --> 00:13:34.930
When it goes into your vest,
so you got the pressurized,

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:37.872
you know, mixture, whatever is in there.

00:13:37.872 --> 00:13:40.834
And then when it goes into the vest,

00:13:40.834 --> 00:13:44.385
it creates a buoyancy that
wasn't a force that was existing

00:13:44.385 --> 00:13:45.449
when it was pressurized.

00:13:45.449 --> 00:13:46.464
That's crazy.

00:13:46.464 --> 00:13:47.655
Yeah.
I didn't know about this.

00:13:47.655 --> 00:13:49.908
I always kind of thought that
they like dropped weights

00:13:49.908 --> 00:13:50.741
or something.

00:13:50.741 --> 00:13:51.732
I knew they didn't, but I don't know like

00:13:51.732 --> 00:13:52.906
how do you get back up?

00:13:52.906 --> 00:13:53.739
Yeah.

00:13:53.739 --> 00:13:56.685
The air mixture, which there's, you know,

00:13:56.685 --> 00:13:58.625
the air we breathe is mostly nitrogen.

00:13:58.625 --> 00:13:59.458
Yeah.

00:13:59.458 --> 00:14:02.055
So that's why when you're down at depth,

00:14:02.055 --> 00:14:06.445
The nitrogen content
in your body increases.

00:14:06.445 --> 00:14:09.256
Do you have to like
dial in what the mix is?

00:14:09.256 --> 00:14:10.201
No, no, no.

00:14:10.201 --> 00:14:11.112
It's all predetermined?

00:14:11.112 --> 00:14:11.945
Yeah.

00:14:11.945 --> 00:14:12.798
It's just a tank.

00:14:12.798 --> 00:14:14.085
You breathe out of it.

00:14:14.085 --> 00:14:14.918
Okay.

00:14:14.918 --> 00:14:16.440
You suck it down.

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:20.466
So yeah, there's this thing
that we had to learn was

00:14:20.466 --> 00:14:21.466
getting in the pool.

00:14:21.466 --> 00:14:22.481
I mean everything.

00:14:22.481 --> 00:14:24.692
How do you put on fins
without killing yourself?

00:14:24.692 --> 00:14:28.330
How do you put on your goggles, You know,

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:29.163
in a way that they-

00:14:29.163 --> 00:14:30.375
And then they fall back into the water,

00:14:30.375 --> 00:14:31.208
is that what you did?

00:14:31.208 --> 00:14:32.041
Did you do that inside the pool?

00:14:32.041 --> 00:14:32.874
Well, we walk into the pool.

00:14:32.874 --> 00:14:35.128
We did one of those like
long step into the pool,

00:14:35.128 --> 00:14:38.439
but we didn't do the like backflip

00:14:38.439 --> 00:14:40.875
off the side of a dingy into the pool.

00:14:40.875 --> 00:14:42.121
You'll do that next time?

00:14:42.121 --> 00:14:42.954
You'll do that this weekend?

00:14:42.954 --> 00:14:44.280
That's the one thing that I,

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:45.553
I don't know.

00:14:45.553 --> 00:14:47.832
Oh you're like, "I don't
wanna do the back clip thing."

00:14:47.832 --> 00:14:49.275
But over the course of the four hours,

00:14:49.275 --> 00:14:50.108
like the first thing to do is all right,

00:14:50.108 --> 00:14:52.174
you're gonna start breathing underwater.

00:14:52.174 --> 00:14:55.307
Let's go down and sit on
the bottom of the pool.

00:14:55.307 --> 00:14:58.609
And, you know, they
say, breath constantly.

00:14:58.609 --> 00:15:02.619
You gotta like breathe calmly in and out.

00:15:02.619 --> 00:15:05.023
And then I get down in there

00:15:05.023 --> 00:15:07.299
and I'm just starting to breathe.

00:15:07.299 --> 00:15:09.518
I mean, there's a little bit of,

00:15:09.518 --> 00:15:13.910
you have to like suck the air
out of the tank a little bit.

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:14.823
It kind of feels that way.

00:15:14.823 --> 00:15:17.953
Like, it's a restricted flow of air.

00:15:17.953 --> 00:15:20.633
It's not just like breathing
air right here right now.

00:15:20.633 --> 00:15:21.618
There's a little resistance.

00:15:21.618 --> 00:15:22.698
You gotta coax it out.

00:15:22.698 --> 00:15:25.195
And then your brain is still telling you,

00:15:25.195 --> 00:15:27.404
this is not a good idea.

00:15:27.404 --> 00:15:28.874
This is a bad idea.

00:15:28.874 --> 00:15:31.863
This is a life-ending
idea to breathe down here

00:15:31.863 --> 00:15:32.983
under this water.

00:15:32.983 --> 00:15:34.373
Don't do it.

00:15:34.373 --> 00:15:37.486
And so at a certain point,
very early on for me,

00:15:37.486 --> 00:15:40.726
that sensation started to creep back in,

00:15:40.726 --> 00:15:43.952
and then he's like, come
up, telling me thumbs up.

00:15:43.952 --> 00:15:44.785
So I come back up.

00:15:44.785 --> 00:15:45.618
He could tell?

00:15:45.618 --> 00:15:46.451
No, he's like, "How you doing?"

00:15:46.451 --> 00:15:47.762
I'm like, "I'm doing pretty good.

00:15:47.762 --> 00:15:48.605
Just taking an adjustment."

00:15:48.605 --> 00:15:50.298
He's like, "You guys are doing great.

00:15:50.298 --> 00:15:53.245
Now we're gonna fill up your
mask halfway with water."

00:15:53.245 --> 00:15:55.766
And I'm gonna teach you
how to get all the water

00:15:55.766 --> 00:15:58.214
outta your mask without coming up for air.

00:15:58.214 --> 00:15:59.047
Yeah, this is it.

00:15:59.047 --> 00:16:00.130
Without coming up to the surface.

00:16:00.130 --> 00:16:02.663
I mean, basically, you
have to learn everything

00:16:02.663 --> 00:16:05.746
that could go wrong or
make you uncomfortable

00:16:05.746 --> 00:16:09.281
or need adjustment that you can fix it

00:16:09.281 --> 00:16:11.572
without giving in to the
urge to try to come up

00:16:11.572 --> 00:16:12.405
to the surface.

00:16:12.405 --> 00:16:15.572
'Cause again, you're 30, 60-feet down.

00:16:16.713 --> 00:16:20.960
You have to get rid of
this notion of, well,

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:23.763
I gotta go to the surface
to take care of this.

00:16:23.763 --> 00:16:24.596
Right.

00:16:24.596 --> 00:16:25.429
No, no, no.

00:16:25.429 --> 00:16:28.147
You gotta take care of it right there.

00:16:28.147 --> 00:16:29.559
How's Lincoln doing at this point?

00:16:29.559 --> 00:16:30.392
Better than me.

00:16:30.392 --> 00:16:31.225
Okay.

00:16:31.225 --> 00:16:32.058
I just wanted to make sure.

00:16:32.058 --> 00:16:35.475
So, he taught us how to clear the mask,

00:16:36.848 --> 00:16:39.084
but we go underwater, he points at me

00:16:39.084 --> 00:16:41.048
and he like does a mask sign,

00:16:41.048 --> 00:16:42.930
like fill up your mask with water.

00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:45.828
So, you seal the top of the mask.

00:16:45.828 --> 00:16:48.561
Well, first of all, you let
water in the top of the mask.

00:16:48.561 --> 00:16:49.818
You just kinda like take it off.

00:16:49.818 --> 00:16:51.236
And then it fills out with water.

00:16:51.236 --> 00:16:52.368
You can fill it up halfway if you wanted

00:16:52.368 --> 00:16:53.642
to kind of ease into it.

00:16:53.642 --> 00:16:54.746
And then you put the mask back on.

00:16:54.746 --> 00:16:59.147
You're like, okay, now you
seal the top of the mask

00:16:59.147 --> 00:17:00.679
against your forehead.

00:17:00.679 --> 00:17:03.930
And you look up towards the surface

00:17:03.930 --> 00:17:07.332
and you raise the bottom of the mask out.

00:17:07.332 --> 00:17:08.165
And blow?

00:17:08.165 --> 00:17:10.123
And blow and then you
blow outta your nose.

00:17:10.123 --> 00:17:10.956
Yeah.

00:17:10.956 --> 00:17:12.149
Which of course is part of the mask.

00:17:12.149 --> 00:17:16.673
And then the air from your nose
fills up the top of the mask

00:17:16.673 --> 00:17:19.924
and down, down, down,
forcing all the water out

00:17:19.924 --> 00:17:21.484
and then you can put the mask back on.

00:17:21.484 --> 00:17:23.419
It's a freaking magic trick.

00:17:23.419 --> 00:17:24.643
Did you know this?

00:17:24.643 --> 00:17:26.623
I didn't know it, but
before you explained it,

00:17:26.623 --> 00:17:29.397
I would have guessed
that that's what you did.

00:17:29.397 --> 00:17:30.230
Yeah.

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:32.317
But you've never done
it, when you've snorkeled

00:17:32.317 --> 00:17:33.305
you've never done it.

00:17:33.305 --> 00:17:35.029
No, 'cause I haven't been-

00:17:35.029 --> 00:17:35.862
Yeah.

00:17:35.862 --> 00:17:37.315
'Cause, you can come up
to the surface and clear,

00:17:37.315 --> 00:17:39.497
and we also had to learn
to clear your snorkel

00:17:39.497 --> 00:17:41.583
and stuff I didn't know.

00:17:41.583 --> 00:17:44.009
But the first time I did that,

00:17:44.009 --> 00:17:46.521
like my mask is filled up with water.

00:17:46.521 --> 00:17:47.862
I'm opening my eyes in my pool.

00:17:47.862 --> 00:17:50.280
I'm like, "I don't like to
open my eyes in my pool."

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:52.162
And then I'm like, "Oh,
I forgot to breathe."

00:17:52.162 --> 00:17:54.532
And then I take a big
deep breath and I'm like,

00:17:54.532 --> 00:17:56.292
and then like my lungs are filled with air

00:17:56.292 --> 00:17:58.307
and I breathe out just a little bit.

00:17:58.307 --> 00:17:59.333
And then I breathe in again.

00:17:59.333 --> 00:18:00.846
And all of a sudden I'm
like having this panic-

00:18:00.846 --> 00:18:03.653
Why are you making me panic
by telling your story?

00:18:03.653 --> 00:18:04.695
Like you're in the pool.

00:18:04.695 --> 00:18:06.274
Because it was freaking scary.

00:18:06.274 --> 00:18:09.942
'Cause I was like, I
packed my lungs with air

00:18:09.942 --> 00:18:13.840
and I just felt like I kept
trying to pack more air in there

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:18.757
and I started panicking and
then it's like I had to come up

00:18:20.066 --> 00:18:24.450
and then I was, you know,
took a breath at the surface

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:25.283
and then I was like, "All right,

00:18:25.283 --> 00:18:26.561
I gotta get this under control.

00:18:26.561 --> 00:18:28.610
I gotta calm down."

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:31.020
And I talked to my instructor about it

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:32.799
and he gave me some tips about like,

00:18:32.799 --> 00:18:35.684
make sure you breathe
out, empty your lungs.

00:18:35.684 --> 00:18:37.242
Like breathe out all the way

00:18:37.242 --> 00:18:39.792
before you start breathing in again.

00:18:39.792 --> 00:18:43.042
And I just had to start telling myself,

00:18:44.395 --> 00:18:45.682
"Doesn't matter what
happens with your mask

00:18:45.682 --> 00:18:46.761
or anything like that.

00:18:46.761 --> 00:18:51.050
As long as you're breathing,
you can handle everything else

00:18:51.050 --> 00:18:54.813
and you don't need to get
worked up here, stay calm."

00:18:54.813 --> 00:18:59.813
It's a meditative exercise of
discipline to train your brain

00:18:59.937 --> 00:19:02.416
and your instincts to
turn off in certain ways

00:19:02.416 --> 00:19:03.923
and to stay chill.

00:19:03.923 --> 00:19:07.336
Because you know, even
if you start sucking down

00:19:07.336 --> 00:19:09.834
a lot of air, it's just gonna
limit the amount of time

00:19:09.834 --> 00:19:11.653
you're able to enjoy your dive anyway,

00:19:11.653 --> 00:19:14.581
'cause you're gonna run outta air.

00:19:14.581 --> 00:19:16.081
Or die from panic.

00:19:17.530 --> 00:19:19.082
I gotta say, I'm a
little worried about you.

00:19:19.082 --> 00:19:20.223
I got used to that.

00:19:20.223 --> 00:19:22.447
And then he is like, "Now we're
gonna lose your regulator.

00:19:22.447 --> 00:19:27.447
You're gonna lose the thing
that is keeping you alive.

00:19:27.716 --> 00:19:30.108
You're gonna lose the hose."

00:19:30.108 --> 00:19:31.821
And then he taught us how to find it

00:19:31.821 --> 00:19:33.123
and put it back in our mouths.

00:19:33.123 --> 00:19:35.594
And like, that was scary 'cause like now

00:19:35.594 --> 00:19:36.523
I am holding my breath and he's like,

00:19:36.523 --> 00:19:37.706
"Don't hold your breath,

00:19:37.706 --> 00:19:39.709
you have to be breathing out constantly."

00:19:39.709 --> 00:19:42.462
So I'm breathing out
assuming that I'm gonna find

00:19:42.462 --> 00:19:44.466
the regulator to put back in my mouth.

00:19:44.466 --> 00:19:45.299
Okay.

00:19:45.299 --> 00:19:47.242
So you're saying that you
can't hold your breath

00:19:47.242 --> 00:19:50.026
at any point because even if
you're not moving vertical-

00:19:50.026 --> 00:19:53.497
You technically can but
when you're learning

00:19:53.497 --> 00:19:56.848
you need to develop a discipline
of constantly breathing.

00:19:56.848 --> 00:19:57.937
Just so you don't forget, make
it a reflex kind of thing.

00:19:57.937 --> 00:19:58.770
Yeah.

00:19:58.770 --> 00:19:59.603
Yeah.

00:19:59.603 --> 00:20:00.769
'Cause when you're panicking
and if you have to do

00:20:00.769 --> 00:20:03.055
an emergency ascent to the surface-

00:20:03.055 --> 00:20:03.888
You wanna keep it coming out.

00:20:03.888 --> 00:20:07.721
You have to keep your
airway open by breathing out

00:20:07.721 --> 00:20:10.471
so that your lungs don't explode.

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:15.270
So we did that and like put in a regulator

00:20:17.309 --> 00:20:19.159
and then it's like practice
losing your regulator

00:20:19.159 --> 00:20:21.316
and then using your backup regulator.

00:20:21.316 --> 00:20:24.163
And now it's like, all
right, now let's practice

00:20:24.163 --> 00:20:26.168
the symbol for I'm out of air,

00:20:26.168 --> 00:20:29.666
which is like slitting
your own throat symbol.

00:20:29.666 --> 00:20:32.734
And so then it's like Lincoln
and I had to rescue each other

00:20:32.734 --> 00:20:35.015
underwater by sharing our
